<USER_REQUEST>
What is the definition of whole body oxygen delivery, and how is it calculated?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Full body oxygen delivery is the process by which oxygen is transported from the lungs to the tissues, where it is used for metabolic processes. It is calculated by multiplying the oxygen delivery rate (O2DO2) with the oxygen content (O2sat). The formula for full body oxygen delivery is:  
\[ \text{VO}_2 = \text{CO} \times \text{Sa} \times \text{Hgb} \times \text{Hct} \]  
Where:  
- \(\text{VO}_2\) is the full body oxygen delivery (mL O2/min).  
- \(\text{CO}\) is the cardiac output (mL/min).  
- \(\text{Sa}\) is the arterial oxygen saturation (%).  
- \(\text{Hgb}\) is the hemoglobin concentration (g/dL).  
- \(\text{Hct}\) is the hematocrit (%).  

Each component of the equation is calculated as follows:  
- \(\text{CO} = \text{HR} \times \text{SV}\), where \(\text{HR}\) is the heart rate and \(\text{SV}\) is the stroke volume.  
- \(\text{Sa}\) is measured using a pulse oximeter or blood gas analysis.  
- \(\text{Hgb}\) is measured using a blood test.  
- \(\text{Hct}\) is measured using a blood test.  

The full body oxygen delivery is calculated by multiplying the components of the equation. For example, if the cardiac output is 1000 mL/min, the arterial oxygen saturation is 90%, the hemoglobin concentration is 11 g/dL, and the hematocrit is 45%, then the full body oxygen delivery is:  
\[ \text{VO}_2 = 1000 \times 0.90 \times 11 \times 0.45 = 450 \text{ mL O}_2/\text{min} \]  

The full body oxygen delivery is an important parameter in the management of critically ill patients, as it can help to identify patients at risk for tissue ischemia.
