","To calculate the standard enthalpy change for the reaction, we use the formula:

\[
\Delta H = \sum n \times \Delta H_f(products) - \sum n \times \Delta H_f(reactants)
\]

where:
- \(\Delta H\) is the standard enthalpy change for the reaction.
- \(\Delta H_f\) is the standard enthalpy of formation for each substance.
- \(n\) is the stoichiometric coefficient of each substance in the balanced equation.

In the given reaction:
\[
\text{CaO(s] + \text{H}_2\text{O}(l) \rightarrow \text{Ca(OH]2(s]}
\]
The stoichiometric coefficients are:
- \(n_{\text{CaO}} = 1\)
- \([ n_{\text{H}_2\text{O}} = 1 \)
- \(n_{\text{Ca(OH}_2} = 1\)

The standard enthalpies of formation are:
- \(\Delta H_f(\text{CaO}) = -635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}\)
- \(\Delta H_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) = -285.8 \, \text{kJ/mol}\)
- \(\Delta H_f(\text{Ca(OH}_2} = -986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}\)

Substituting these values into the formula:

\[
\Delta H = 1 \cdot \Delta H_f(\text{Ca(OH]_2}) - ( 1 \cdot \Delta H_f(\text{CaO}) + 1 \cdot \Delta H_f(\text{H}_2\text{O}) )
\]
\[
\Delta H = 1 \cdot (-986.2 \, \text{kJ/mol}) - ( 1 \cdot (-635.1 \, \text{kJ/mol}) + 1 \cdot (-285.8 \, \text{kJ/mol}) )
\]
