- The system's reliability is the product of the reliability functions of its components:  
\[ R_S(t) = R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot R_3(t) \cdot R_4(t) \cdot R_5(t) \]  
This is because the system only survives if all components survive.  

**b) Parallel Structure**  
In a parallel series, the system survives if at least one component survives.  
- Let \( R_1(t) \), \( R_2(t) \), \( R_3(t) \), \( R_4(t) \), and \( R_5(t) \) be the reliability functions of the five components.  
- The failure rate of the system is the sum of the failure rates of its components.  
- The system's reliability is the probability that none of the components fail, which is the complement of the probability that all components fail.  
\[ R_S(t) = P(\text{No failure}) = P(\text{Component 1 survives}) \times P(\text{Component 2 survives}) \times P(\text{Component 3 survives}) \times P(\text{Component 4 survives}) \times P(\text{Component 5 survives)} \]  
\[ R_S(t) = R_1(t) \cdot R_2(t) \cdot R_3(t) \times R_4(t) \times R_5(t) \]  
This is the same as the serial system, but the interpretation is different.  

