The detection of EC reactions in CV is achieved by observing the electrochemical behavior of the intermediate E species and the final product. The CV results depend on the relative kinetics of the E and C steps. For example, if the chemical reaction (C) is much faster than the electron transfer (E) step, the CV will resemble a linear voltograph with a single peak. If the chemical reaction (C) is much slower than the electron transfer (E) step, the CV will show a peak for the E species, and the chemical reaction (C) will not be observed. If the two reactions have similar kinetics, the CV will show a more complex peak shape. 

