","The selective reduction of the aldehyde group in a molecule containing an ester and a chloromethyl group (e.g., 156 in Scheme 22.73) is achieved by using a reducing agent that selectively targets the carbonyl group of the aldehyde while leaving the ester and chloromethyl groups intact. The correct choice of reducing agent is critical because:
1. Esters are more resistant to reduction than aldehydes.
2. Chloromethyl groups are susceptible to deprotonation or displacement, which can lead to side reactions if the reducing agent is too strong.

The correct reducing agent for this selective reduction is **sodium borate (Na₂BO₄O₆) in ethanol**. This is part of the **Corey-Schultz reduction**, which is a mild and selective method for reducing aldehydes in the presence of other functional groups like esters and chloromethyl groups. 

\boxed{
\begin{aligned}
&\text{The Corey-Schultz reduction involves the reaction of sodium borate (Na}_2\text{BO}_4\text{O}_6) with ethanol to form a borite intermediate, which is a milder reducing agent than sodium borate alone.} \\
&\text{The borite selectively reduces the aldehyde group to an alcohol, while the ester and chloromethyl groups remain unchanged.}
\end{aligned}
}

The reaction proceeds as follows:
\[
\text{Na}_2\text{BO}_4\text{O}_6 + 2 \text{EtOH} \rightleftharpoons \text{B(2O)}_2 + 2 Na^+ + 2 EtOH \rightarrow \text{B(2O)}_2 \text{ + 2 EtOH} \rightarrow \text{Borite intermediate}
\]
The borite intermediate then reacts with the aldehyde to form the alcohol.

