<USER_REQUEST>
What type of carbonyl compound is primarily produced when Grignard reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂)?
</USER_REQUEST>

<ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>
Grignoid reagents react with carbon dioxide (CO₂) to produce **carboxylic acids**. The reaction proceeds as follows:

1. The Grignard reagent acts as a nucleophile, attacking the carbonyl carbon of CO₂.
2. This forms an enolate intermediate.
3. The enolate is then protonated by water (from the aqueous work-up) to form the corresponding carboxylic acid.

Thus, the product is a carboxylic acid with a side chain derived from the Grignard reagent.
</ASSISTANT_RESPONSE>

