","The ability of polymer-based smart materials to respond to specific stimuli is achieved by incorporating functional groups or molecular units that respond to the desired stimulus. These materials can be tuned by adjusting the concentration of these functional groups, the polymer backbone, or the overall architecture of the material. For example, temperature-responsive polymers can be tuned by varying the length of the polymer chain or the type of monomer used. The following properties of polymer-based smart materials can be tuned to respond to specific stimuli:

1. Temperature: The temperature-responsive behavior of polymer-based smart materials can be tuned by adjusting the glass transition temperature (Tg) or the lower-transition temperature (LCST). This can be achieved by varying the length of the polymer chain, the type of monomer used, or the presence of additives such as surfactants or salts. For example, poly(4-hydroxyacrylic acid) (PHAA) has a LCST of around 37°C, which can be lowered by adding salts or surfactants.

2. pH: The pH-responsive behavior of polymer-based smart materials can be tuned by adjusting the concentration of functional groups that can be protonated or deprotonated at specific pH values. For example, poly(4-hydroxymethylacrylic acid) (PHMAA) has a LCST of around37°C, which can be lowered by adding salts or surfactants.

3. Light: The light-responsive behavior of polymer-based smart materials can be tuned by adjusting the concentration of photosensitive groups that can undergo reversible reactions upon exposure to light. For example, photo-degradable polymers can be tuned by varying the concentration of photo-degradable groups.

