","The decay length of an evanescent wave is given by the inverse of the difference between the refractive indices of the two media. For example, when light from a material with refractive index \(n_1\) enters a material with refractive index \(n_2\), the decay length \(L\) of the evanescent wave is given by:

\[
L = \frac{\lambda}{2\pi \left(n_1 - n_2\right)}
\]

In this case, the evanescent wave is generated due to the difference in refractive indices between the core (GaAs) and the cladding (Ga\(_{0.2}\)Al\(_{0.8}\)Al). The evanescent wave decays in the cladding. The given condition is \(n_{\text{eff}} - n_f \sim 0.3\), where \(n_{\text{eff}}\) is the refractive index of the cladding and \(n_f\) is the refractive index of the core.

Thus, the difference in refractive indices is:

\[
n_{\text{eff}} - n_f = 0.3
\]

The wavelength is given as \(l = 1 \, \mu\text{m} = 1 \times 1{0^{-6}\text{ m}}\).

Substituting these values into the formula for the decay length:

\[
L = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{2\pi (0.3)} = \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{0.6 \pi} \approx \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{0.6 \times 3.17} \approx \frac{1 \times 10^{-6}}{1.91} \approx 0.525 \times 10^{-6} \, \text{m} = 525 \, \mu\text{m}
\]

Thus, the decay length is approximately \(525 \, \mu\text{m}\).

Final answer:
\[
\boxed{525 \, \mu\text{m}}
\]",relevance,"# Task Description
