To calculate the recoil velocity of the Earth, we use the principle of momentum conservation. The recoil velocity is small, so we can approximate the process as a head-on collision. The initial momentum of the system is zero, as the Earth and the cannonball are both at rest before the collision. After the collision, the cannonball has a velocity \(v_k\) and the Earth has a recoil velocity \(v_E\). The final momentum of the system is the sum of the momentum of the cannonball and the momentum of the Earth.

The principle of momentum conservation states that the initial momentum equals the final momentum:
\[
\Delta p = delta \Delta m = 0 = m_k v_k + m_E v_E
\]
where:
- \(m_k\) is the mass of the cannonball (1 kg)
- \(v_k\) is the velocity of the cannonball (+90.0 m/s)
- \(m_E\) is the mass of the Earth (\(5.97 \times 10^24\) kg)
- \(v_E\) is the recoil velocity of the Earth (negative, as it is opposite to the cannonball's velocity)

Substituting the given values:
\[
0 = (1 \text{ kg})(90.0 \text{ m/s}) + (5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}) v_E
\]
Solving for \(v_E\):
\[
v_E = -\frac{1 \text{ kg} \times 90.0 \text{ m/s}}{5.97 \times 10^{24} \text{ kg}}
\]
\[
v_E = -\frac{90.0}{5.97 \times 10^{24}} \text{ m/s}
\]
\[
v_E \approx -1.5 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}
\]

The recoil velocity of the Earth is approximately \(-1.5 \times 10^{-22} \text{ m/s}\). The negative sign indicates that the Earth moves in the opposite direction of the cannonball. The magnitude is very small, as expected.

