","1. **Calculate the standard free energy change for the hydrolysis of ATP**:
   - The hydrolysis of ATP is: ATP + Pi → ADP + Pi
   - The standard free energy change for ATP hydrolysis (ΔΔG) is calculated using the change in free energy for the reverse reaction:
   - ΔΔG = -(-30.5 kJ/mol) = 30.5 kJ/mol (for ATP + Pi → ADP + Pi)
   - However, the standard free energy change depends on the concentrations of ATP, ADP, and Pi. The standard free energy change (ΔG) is related to the standard free energy change at standard conditions (ΔΔG) by the van't equation:
     \[
     \Delta G = \Delta \Delta G - RT \ln \left( \frac{[\text{ADP}] \cdot [\text{Pi}]}{\text{ATP} \text{ (standard conditions)}} \right)
     \]
   - At 37°C (310 K), the standard concentration of ATP is 1 mM, and the concentrations of ADP and Pi are 1 mM.
   - Substituting the values:
     \[
     \Delta G = 30.5 \, \text{kJ/mol} - 1.0 \times text{J/mol·K} \times 310 \, \text{K} \times \ln \left( \frac{1 \, \text{mM} \times 1 \, \text{mM}}{1 \, \text{mM}} \right)
     \]
     \[
     \Delta G = 30.5 \, \text{kJ/mol} - 310 \, \text{J/K} \times 310 \, \text{K} \times \ln (1)
     \]
     \[
     \Delta G = 30.5 \, \text{kJ/mol} - 310 \, \text{J/K} \times 310 \, \text{K} \times 0 = 30.5 \, \text{kJ/mol}
     \]
